the chaplain of the House of  Representatives who had been forced out  by House Speaker Paul Ryan will now stay  on the job father Patrick Conroy had  agreed to step down later this month at  the speaker's request but in a letter  yesterday the 67 year old Roman Catholic  priest told Ryan that after consulting a  lawyer he was rescinding his resignation  Ryan says Conroy will remain in his  position ed O'Keefe is on Capitol Hill  with how this unlikely dispute unfolded  and then this latest turn ed good  morning good morning John this whole  situation causing a lot of bipartisan  head-scratching members of both parties  want to know why exactly  Conroy was asked to resign the chaplain  himself now suggests so the prayer he  gave last fall during the GOP tax reform  debate may have been seen by the  speaker's office to be too political I'm  not coming a defiant father Patrick  Conroy resurrected his tenure as House  chaplain yesterday afternoon issuing  this searing two-page resignation  retraction letter to House Speaker Paul  Ryan saying I have never been  disciplined nor reprimanded nor have I  ever heard a complaint about my ministry  during my time as House chaplain some of  Conroy's defenders believe the  controversy dates back to a prayer he  delivered in November during debate over  the GOP tax reform plan may their  efforts these days guarantee that there  are not winners and losers under new tax  laws but benefits balanced and shared by  all on Monday at an event in Milwaukee  Ryan defended his decision a number of  our members felt like the pastoral  services were not being adequately  served but father Conroy states in his  letter that when Ryan's chief of staff  Jonathan Burks asked for his resignation  he mentioned my November prayer and made  a dismissive comment something like  maybe it's time we had a chaplain that  wasn't a Catholic Burke's disputed those  statements saying I am disappointed by  the misunderstanding but wish him the  best as he continues to serve the house  any suggestion that the Catholics need  not apply is deeply offensive to  Virginia Democratic congressman Gerry  Connolly who is cast  says reaction to Conroy's ouster in the  house was nearly universal that went  from shock to outrage and I might say  not just Democrats lots of Republicans  as well House Minority Leader Nancy  Pelosi applauded the decision but says  she wants to know more about this whole  situation and in his statement  announcing Conroy's reinstatement the  speaker said he wanted to avoid a  drawn-out fight in hopes to meet with  the chaplain when Congress is back in  town next week Gail all right thank you  very much
 

